{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"(allow-not-applicable|tooling-slice-id|v1\\\\.2\\\\.0|D-044|Invocation-log convention extension|rendered-template log entry|MANIFEST\\\\.json)\\\" scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh docs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md docs/DECISIONS.md docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-correctness.json docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"docs/DECISIONS.md:3:**Last Updated:** 2026-05-09 (D-044 filed for WP-SCP-024 024B-core; estate cascade scope split)\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:29:> **D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 024A):**\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:30:> D-044 was reserved for WP-SCP-024 slice 024B (`scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh`\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:44:> assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:89:| D-041 | 2026-05-03 | Adopt the cross-repo scorecard data shape (`schemas/scorecard-emit.schema.json` v0.1) + opt-in adopter participation model per WP-SCP-023 023B (this PR). The schema defines aggregated per-rule counts (raw_findings, denies, waived, rule_config_disabled), waivers aggregate (active_count, by_rule_id, expiring_within_30d), rule-config aggregate (disabled_rules, expiring_within_30d), repo / ref / commit / scp_version / RFC3339 emitted_at, and a verdict (allow/deny/warn). The schema EXPLICITLY DOES NOT include waiver content (`reason`, `approved_by`, `waiver_id` strings) — invariant 2 of WP-SCP-023 plan-doc binds this. The reusable workflow gains a `scorecard-emit: bool` `workflow_call` input (default false) so existing adopters see no behaviour change; opt-in adopters set `with: scorecard-emit: true`. Schema versioning: MAJOR-pinned per `policies/VERSIONING.md` D-036 + WP-SCP-023 plan-doc §10 Q1 — aggregator drops emits with unknown `schema_version`. Verification: GitHub Actions Artifact Attestation (OIDC-based) via `actions/attest-build-provenance@v4.1.0` (SHA `a2bbfa25...`) at emit time; aggregator verifies via `gh attestation verify --signer-workflow` (per D-042 to be filed at slice 023C). v1.2.0 cut on this slice's merge per VERSIONING.md MINOR category | ACCEPTED | This is the first slice of WP-SCP-023's implementation chain (023A plan-doc landed at PR #97, commit `7075234`, 2026-05-03 R4 fixpoint). The data shape was specified at plan-doc level; this slice ratifies it as code surface. Default-false on the workflow input is the load-bearing safety property: existing adopters cannot have new behaviour spring on them; opt-in is explicit per invariant 4. The waiver-content exclusion (invariant 2) is enforced by the schema's `additionalProperties: false` constraint on every level + the explicit field enumeration. The MAJOR-pinning posture means schema breakage requires v2.0.0 + adopter migration; v0.1 prefix on `schema_version` reflects the freedom to amend until first adopter opts in. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: aggregator runs on the SCP repo with single-operator identity (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-023 023A plan-doc §9 D-041 reservation |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:92:| D-044 | 2026-05-09 | Adopt the WP-SCP-024 slice 024B-core operational contracts for cascade scaffolding. Components: (1) `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder` per WP-SCP-020 §11 follow-up + WP-SCP-021 follow-up `SCP-075-scaffolder-compose`) generates adopter-side artefacts mechanically - bootstrap-only (refuses `CI=true` / `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` per D-035 symmetry); validates `--scp-sha` against SCP main HEAD lineage (warns if not main HEAD; refuses if non-existent); emits a TF-023E-002 warning for post-v1.2.0 SHAs while recommending the v1.0.0 pin; `--scp-sha` 40-char hex regex; `--default-branch` enum {main, master, develop}; `--scorecard-emit` enum; `--output-dir` writability + path-normalised system-path denylist (incl `/private/var/*` macOS canonicalisation); emits `MANIFEST.json` with SHA256 audit; arguments per plan-doc §5.3. Canonical adopter slugs are normalization aids only, not collision-resistant IDs, so cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization. (2) `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` is the canonical adopter wrapper shape per ADOPT-001 §12 with Renovate inline-comment marker `# renovate: datasource=github-tags depName=jrnb2024/standards-control-plane` enabling auto-bump per plan-doc invariant 8 Threshold A criterion; substitutions `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, `{{SCP_SHA}}`, `{{SCORECARD_EMIT}}`; least-privilege workflow-level permissions (`contents:read` + `statuses:write` only). (3) `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` CLI tool implements plan-doc invariant 2 cascade-status CI enforcement: 4 enforcement behaviours (fail-closed default for absent/unrecognised; `onboarded` -> log-entry-modified + target-match; `onboarded-operator-bump` -> log entry + `STATUS.md` `TF-024X-renovate-<slug>` row matching invariant 2 regex literally; `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` -> `DISPATCH-NOTE` `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference + log file NOT modified) + explicit tooling-slice opt-in gate for `cascade-status: not applicable` (`--allow-not-applicable`; default refusal for direct CLI use). Regex via `python3 re.search()` (PCRE-style); exactly-one-match assertions on both `cascade-status` and `**Target:**` extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses; adopter-slug canonicalisation per plan-doc spec. CLI tool only - GitHub Actions workflow wiring (`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml`) defers to slice 024B-extras alongside `enable-required-check.sh --restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass procedure. D-044 was reserved for slice 024B at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge (PR #102, 2026-05-04); after the operator-decided scope-split (SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md), D-044 stays scoped to core deliverables; sibling D-NNN may be reserved at 024B-extras authoring time for the deferred surface (`--restore` + break-glass + workflow wiring). Slice 024B-core ships under documented protocol deviation per feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md note + justify rule (kernel hook lives in ACC repo only; per-repo install pattern is FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 - open). | ACCEPTED | D-044 was reserved at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc 024A merge per the §9 reservation block. The original 024B accumulated 7 fix-rounds + 7 R-rounds against a single branch with each round closing 5-6 MAJ but introducing 2-3 new defects in just-added code (R6 missed primary-deliverable workflow wiring; R7 fix introduced bootstrap defect on slice's own PR). Empirical signal: surface area too large (16 files, ~3500 lines added) for single-pass convergence. Operator decided 2026-05-09 to split 024B into 024B-core (this slice; ~5 files; scaffolder + template + CI enforcement script + tests) + 024B-extras (deferred; `--restore` mode + ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass + `.github/workflows/` CI wiring + step 7 operator-interactive demo). Split is incremental delivery, NOT descope - both halves still ship; both required before 024C cohort onboarding opens. Original 024B work preserved at `feature/wp-scp-024-024b-extras-parking` branch (audit trail: 7 codex-fix-round-N-audit.log files + 7 R-round archive directories incl r8-archive carry-forward findings). Slice 024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder generates adopter artefacts; CI enforcement script (CLI) validates cascade-status declarations; tests cover both. The CLI script can be invoked manually as a stop-gap until 024B-extras wires it into GitHub Actions. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged (D-031); 2026-07-21 quarterly review covers. Identified at WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation |\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-completeness_governance.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":370408,\"duration_api_ms\":370852,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"I now have all the files needed. Let me perform the completeness governance analysis and produce the structured findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"Sonnet-R1-completeness_governance\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"COMPLETENESS_GOVERNANCE\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"APPROVED_WITH_FINDINGS\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"024B-core scope is internally coherent: scaffolder, adopter wrapper template, cascade-status CI enforcement script (CLI), tests, D-044, STATUS.md chain entry, plan-doc §6 split-note amendment, and SCOPE-CORRECTION are all present. Invariant 2's four CI behaviours are implemented byte-for-byte against the plan-doc regex spec. D-044 is correctly filed in DECISIONS.md scoped to core deliverables; extras-parking branch is referenced; FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 and TF-024B-SCAFFOLDER-023E002-UPDATE are tracked in STATUS.md with 024C gate language. SCOPE-CORRECTION empirically justifies the split with the R6→R7→R8 trajectory table and addresses the anti-descope concern. Anti-descoping check passes: no --restore content, no .github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml, no §12.8 break-glass content detected in any core file. VERSIONING.md alignment confirmed — scaffolder, template, and CI script are internal-only surfaces; no version bump required. One MAJ: plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation row is internally inconsistent — it was partially updated to include 'MANIFEST.json audit emission' but the now-superseded 'Invocation-log convention extension: scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log' was not removed. This creates a false unimplemented-AC signal for future correctness reviewers. Two MIN: §5.3 'Emits:' list undercounts the scaffolder output (3 items listed, 4 emitted), and SCOPE-CORRECTION doesn't contain the invocation-log→MANIFEST.json reframe it's cited for in §5.3. No CRIT. All TF entries applicable to core scope are preserved; extras-only TFs correctly travel with 024B-extras.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-MAJ-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"plan-doc §9 D-044 reservation row is internally inconsistent: it asserts both 'MANIFEST.json audit emission' (accurate) AND the superseded 'Invocation-log convention extension: scaffolder writes a rendered-template log entry alongside enable-required-check.sh's branch-protection log' (not implemented; reframed to MANIFEST.json per §5.3). The row was partially updated but the old claim was not removed.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"plan-doc §9 D-044 Topic cell contains both statements verbatim; scaffold-downstream.sh emits MANIFEST.json only and never writes to branch-protection-log.md; §5.3 amendment note explicitly states MANIFEST.json is the reframed form of the original §9 invocation-log language; DECISIONS.md D-044 ACCEPTED text correctly describes MANIFEST.json only.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Future correctness reviewers at 024C+ reading §9 as the spec will flag the absence of a rendered-template log entry alongside branch-protection-log.md as a missing AC, generating false MAJ findings and consuming fix-round budget unnecessarily.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Remove the 'Invocation-log convention extension' sentence from §9 D-044 Topic cell; replace with a note that MANIFEST.json is the accepted audit surface per the §5.3 reframe decision, consistent with the DECISIONS.md D-044 ACCEPTED text.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-MIN-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"plan-doc §5.3 'Emits:' bullet list contains only 3 files; MANIFEST.json is the 4th file emitted by the scaffolder but is absent from the 'Emits:' block.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"plan-doc §5.3 'Emits:' has 3 bullets (policy-check-wrapper.yml, CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt, CASCADE-PR-BODY.md); scaffold-downstream.sh lines 172-174 + 217-240 emit MANIFEST.json as a 4th file; test_scaffold_downstream.sh validates 4 files including MANIFEST.json.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A reader stopping at the 'Emits:' list expects 3 output files from the scaffolder. The MANIFEST.json mention in the D-044 ratification inline partially mitigates but requires reading beyond the list.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add '<output-dir>/MANIFEST.json' as a 4th bullet in the §5.3 'Emits:' list, matching the actual scaffolder output and the test assertion.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-MIN-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"plan-doc §5.3 cites SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md as the justification source for the invocation-log-convention-extension → MANIFEST.json reframe, but SCOPE-CORRECTION contains no mention of this reframe.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"plan-doc §5.3: '024B-core reframed that audit surface on 2026-05-09 per docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md'; SCOPE-CORRECTION covers only the scope split (scaffolder+template+CI-script vs extras) with no mention of the invocation-log convention extension or its reframe to MANIFEST.json.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An auditor following the §5.3 citation to SCOPE-CORRECTION to understand the reframe rationale finds nothing there. The reframe's primary governance documentation is the §5.3 text itself, not the cited source, creating a broken reference chain.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a brief subsection to SCOPE-CORRECTION documenting the invocation-log-convention-extension → MANIFEST.json reframe decision; OR amend the §5.3 citation to read 'per §5.3 amendment decision at 024B-core authoring time' without referencing SCOPE-CORRECTION.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-nit-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"plan-doc §6 024C AC column does not enumerate the three-part gate (024B-core merged + 024B-extras merged + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closed); the gate is documented only in the split note below the table and in STATUS.md tracked-forward items.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"plan-doc §6 024C AC column lists only 'PIM required-check live; ≥1 Renovate-bump-propagation cycle merged clean; D-045 filed'; split note below table: '024C remains gated on both halves merging, plus FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closure'; STATUS.md: 'FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 (open): 024C MUST NOT open until closed'.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A 024C dispatcher reading only the 024C AC row may miss the three-part gate and open the slice prematurely before both halves merge or FUP closes.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Prepend a precondition line to the §6 024C AC column: 'Preconditions: 024B-core merged + 024B-extras merged + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closed.' This makes the gate machine-checkable from the table without requiring cross-reference to the split note.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"CG-nit-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"CASCADE-PR-BODY.md template emitted by the scaffolder does not prompt the cascade operator to check for and incorporate CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt into the adopter PR if the adopter has a CODEOWNERS file.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scaffold-downstream.sh emits CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt alongside CASCADE-PR-BODY.md; plan-doc §5.2 item 1: 'Optional CODEOWNERS line .github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml @<adopter-CODEOWNERS-account> if the adopter has a CODEOWNERS file'; CASCADE-PR-BODY.md has no CODEOWNERS instruction or reference to the emitted snippet.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A cascade operator at 024C+ may overlook the CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt file and fail to add the CODEOWNERS line for adopters that have a CODEOWNERS file, leaving wrapper ownership undeclared.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a <!-- adopter: review/edit --> comment to CASCADE-PR-BODY.md instructing the operator to merge CODEOWNERS-snippet.txt content into the adopter's CODEOWNERS file if one exists.\\\"}]}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"6108614b-6cbb-48a0-bd77-76d5133843d8\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.8035786,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":5607,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":108056,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":147462,\"output_tokens\":22386,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":108056,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":21523,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":86376,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":29061,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":29061},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1434,\"outputTokens\":17,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001519,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":5607,\"outputTokens\":22386,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":147462,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":108056,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.8020596,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"813a7043-24e4-4333-9563-9937a6e26269\"}\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:14:# docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/DISPATCH-NOTE.md; docs/DECISIONS.md D-044.\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:20:Usage: check-invocation-log-entry.sh --pr <number> | --diff-base <ref> --dispatch-note <path> [--status-md <path>] [--branch-protection-log <path>] [--allow-not-applicable]\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:31:  --allow-not-applicable         Allow cascade-status: not applicable.\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:75:    --allow-not-applicable)\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:180:      die \"ERROR: cascade-status: not applicable found, but --allow-not-applicable was not passed. This carve-out is for tooling slices ONLY (024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, 024G); cohort cascade slices 024C/D/E/F MUST NOT use it. If this is a tooling slice, pass --allow-not-applicable explicitly.\" 2\nscripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh:191:  die \"ERROR: --allow-not-applicable was passed but cascade-status is '$cascade_status' — flag is for tooling slices declaring cascade-status: not applicable ONLY. Cohort cascade slices (024C/D/E/F) MUST NOT pass this flag.\" 2\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":665685,\"duration_api_ms\":666578,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"Now I have all the information needed. Producing the safety bypass review JSON.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"reviewer\\\":\\\"r1-safety_bypass\\\",\\\"lens\\\":\\\"SAFETY_BYPASS\\\",\\\"slice\\\":\\\"WP-SCP-024 024B-core\\\",\\\"date\\\":\\\"2026-05-09\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"The core safety architecture is sound: the cascade-status fail-closed default is correctly wired (unrecognised/absent value → exit 1 before any enforcement); both exactly-one assertions (cascade-status extraction at column-0, **Target:** extraction) close the multi-occurrence injection bypass; the path-traversal denylist runs on the readlink/realpath-normalised OUTPUT_DIR (macOS /private/var/* canonicalisation included); the template permissions are correctly ceiling-scoped (contents:read + statuses:write only, no id-token/attestations at workflow level); the fork-PR refusal if: clause is present and tested; and the blocked-on-adopter-conflict negative assertion (log NOT modified) correctly catches truncation and rename operations (case10b, case20b). The worked-example regex is faithfully implemented byte-for-byte and all 6 matrix entries (✅ex1/ex2, ❌ex3/ex4/ex5/ex6) pass their expected outcomes. The phantom-target-entry exactly-one guard fires correctly at 2 matches. Adopter-slug canonicalization jrnb2024/repo.foo_bar → jrnb2024-repo-foo-bar is correct and tested. The dominant safety gap is the not-applicable cohort-bypass (MAJ-SAFE-001): no machine guard on the CLI prevents a cohort cascade operator from declaring cascade-status: not applicable and passing --allow-not-applicable to exit 0 with zero enforcement. Three MINs surface: slug-collision false-positive matching, TOCTOU between denylist check and file writes, and test TMPDIR residue on SIGKILL. Three nits. No CRITs.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MAJ-SAFE-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"No machine guard prevents a cohort cascade slice (024C–F) from declaring cascade-status: not applicable and invoking the CLI with --allow-not-applicable to exit 0 with zero enforcement. The guard is purely process-level (operator responsibility + R1 review) until 024B-extras CI workflow wiring lands.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 176–183: the 'not applicable' + ALLOW_NOT_APPLICABLE=1 path exits 0 at line 183 before any enforcement block is reached. No subsequent check at lines 190–192 is reached (case exits first). The operator-responsibility carve-out is documented in lines 8–12 and line 180's die-message, but the CLI cannot distinguish cohort slices from tooling slices at invocation time.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Any operator running the CLI for a cohort slice with --allow-not-applicable + a DISPATCH-NOTE declaring cascade-status: not applicable bypasses all three enforcement paths (log-modified check, target-match, STATUS.md TF-row). Since 024B-extras CI wiring is deferred, the gap is live for the entire 024B-core window until 024B-extras merges.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Two options: (1) In-scope CLI hardening: add a required --tooling-slice-id flag alongside --allow-not-applicable, validated against a hardcoded set {024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras, 024G}; cohort operators would need to know a valid ID, raising friction and audit visibility. (2) Out-of-scope (024B-extras): CI workflow wiring enforces that --allow-not-applicable is only passed in the workflow for tooling-slice job conditions. Process mitigation: DISPATCH-NOTE cascade-status: not applicable in a cohort slice is a CRIT R1 correctness finding — flag explicitly in 024C–F dispatch notes.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MIN-SAFE-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"Slug canonicalization collision allows a false-positive STATUS.md TF-row match: if two adopter repos canonicalize to the same slug (e.g. jrnb2024/repo.pim and jrnb2024/repo-pim both → jrnb2024-repo-pim), adopter B's already-present TF-024X-renovate-jrnb2024-repo-pim row satisfies adopter A's onboarded-operator-bump check, making the CI check pass without adopter A having a real tracking row.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"canonicalize_adopter_slug at check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 103–111 maps '.' and '_' to '-'. The adopter-slug regex at line 283 is rf'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}...' — exact slug match. D-044 notes 'cohort repo names must remain unique after canonicalization' but no runtime collision check exists in the CLI or test harness.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future cohort expansion (beyond the current 5 repos) could introduce a slug collision that silently satisfies another adopter's CI check. The current 5-cohort names (pim, recommender, shopify-app, mapp-doc-agent, control-tower) do not collide, but the gap is systemic.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a precomputed collision check in the CI workflow (024B-extras) or in a standalone script that validates adopter-slug uniqueness across the cohort manifest before any cascade slice runs. For now, add a comment in canonicalize_adopter_slug noting the uniqueness pre-condition and document the check as a D-045 gating criterion.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MIN-SAFE-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"TOCTOU window exists between the --output-dir denylist check and the mkdir-p + file-write operations in scaffold-downstream.sh: an attacker with write access to OUTPUT_DIR's parent can swap the directory for a symlink to a denied path after the realpath/denylist check but before mkdir-p follows the (now-swapped) path.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scaffold-downstream.sh: denylist check at lines 143–147 uses the realpath-resolved OUTPUT_DIR. mkdir -p at line 168 uses the same resolved string, but if the directory at that resolved path is replaced by a symlink between lines 147 and 168, mkdir -p follows the new symlink. The resolved string is a real path but the directory entry at that real path can be swapped by a concurrent actor with parent-dir write access.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Low practical risk: bootstrap-only (no CI invocation), operator-controlled local machine. An attacker exploiting this needs local filesystem write access during the narrow window between the denylist check and the first mkdir call. More relevant in shared-workstation environments.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"After mkdir -p, re-verify that the written files are still inside the expected real-path subtree: e.g. verify realpath(wrapper_path) has OUTPUT_DIR as a prefix. Alternatively, open the output directory with O_NOFOLLOW-equivalent semantics via Python's pathlib with follow_symlinks=False checks.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MIN-SAFE-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh creates its TMPDIR inside the repo root (REPO_ROOT/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX). A SIGKILL during the test run leaves temporary directories as untracked files in the git working tree. There is no .gitignore entry protecting against accidental staging or committing of these temp dirs.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 14: TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d ${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX). The EXIT trap at line 15 covers normal exits, INT, and TERM but not SIGKILL. The tmp.scaffold.* glob does not appear in .gitignore. A dev running git add . after a killed test run would stage test artifacts.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"Accidental commit of tmp.scaffold.* directories to the repo. Low-severity in practice (CI should not SIGKILL test processes) but represents a hygiene gap that could surface during iterative local testing.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add tmp.scaffold.* to .gitignore. Additionally, use mktemp -d (system TMPDIR) as test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh does at line 8, or use a TMPDIR=... prefix outside the repo root to avoid working-tree pollution entirely.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"nit-SAFE-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"D-044 trust-model states 'exactly-one-match assertions on both cascade-status and **Target:** extraction close multi-occurrence injection bypasses' without noting that the cascade-status assertion counts only lines at column 0 (the regex uses ^cascade-status: without a leading [ \\\\\\\\t]* prefix). An indented cascade-status: line is invisible to the exactly-one guard.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Python regex at check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 163: r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+...' with re.M — ^ matches start of line; a line like '  cascade-status: onboarded' does not match. test case14 at line 582 confirms indented line is invisible and the single column-0 line is correctly extracted. Contrast with **Target:** regex at line 201 which DOES allow [ \\\\\\\\t]* before the dash.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A reader of D-044 might believe the assertion catches ANY occurrence of 'cascade-status:' in the file. A careless developer writing a DISPATCH-NOTE with cascade-status: in an indented code block (e.g. a YAML example) might expect it to trigger the dual-match guard but it won't. No actual bypass since the column-0 value is still correctly enforced.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a one-line note to D-044: 'The exactly-one assertion counts only column-0 cascade-status: lines (re.M ^ anchor); indented occurrences (e.g. inline examples) are invisible to the guard by design.'\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"nit-SAFE-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh YAML validation is silently skipped when pyyaml is absent: the only test that validates the emitted wrapper is valid YAML exits 0 with a SKIP message rather than failing or using an alternative YAML parser.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 133–134: python3 -c 'import yaml' 2>/dev/null || { echo 'SKIP: pyyaml not installed; YAML validation skipped'; return 0; }. In minimal CI environments (e.g. base ubuntu-24.04 without extras), pyyaml may be absent, silently skipping the only structural YAML validation of the emitted wrapper.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A template change that produces invalid YAML (e.g. indentation error in the uses: line after substitution) would not be caught in environments without pyyaml, breaking every adopter wrapper silently.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a fallback YAML syntax check using python3's built-in PyYAML-free approach (e.g. pyyaml is actually included in standard CPython 3.x environments via the yaml module — verify availability in CI) or use a bash-level YAML linter like yq if available; at minimum document the skip condition in the test comment.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"nit-SAFE-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"SCP_REPO_ROOT environment variable, if set by the operator's shell to a fork of SCP that contains the same template file, redirects the --scp-sha existence check and HEAD-lineage warning to the wrong repository; an invalid SHA against the real SCP repo would pass validation.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"scaffold-downstream.sh line 14: SCP_REPO_ROOT=${SCP_REPO_ROOT:-$(cd ... && pwd)}. Lines 149–165: all git operations use -C $SCP_REPO_ROOT. If SCP_REPO_ROOT points to a fork, git cat-file -e SCP_SHA^{commit} and git rev-parse main both run against the fork. The only identity check for the repo is the template file existence at lines 53–55 — present in any fork.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator with SCP_REPO_ROOT mistakenly set to a fork can scaffold a wrapper pinned to a SHA that exists in the fork but not in the upstream SCP repo, potentially referencing a diverged or experimental commit. Low severity given bootstrap-only context and single-operator estate.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Add a git remote origin URL check: verify git -C $SCP_REPO_ROOT remote get-url origin contains jrnb2024/standards-control-plane (or a configurable expected remote), warning if it doesn't match the expected upstream. Alternative: document the SCP_REPO_ROOT variable in the --help output with a note about the identity assumption.\\\"}],\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\\"fixpoint_gate\\\":\\\"0 CRIT + 0 MAJ required; currently 0 CRIT + 1 MAJ\\\"}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"016b5bc0-e77d-4fa9-b600-e1d655078548\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.0020452,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":7,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":111488,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":197954,\"output_tokens\":34839,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":111488,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":6180,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":88342,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":30527,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":30527},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1838,\"outputTokens\":27,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001973,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":7,\"outputTokens\":34839,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":197954,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":111488,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.0000722,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"39e3708d-d7ed-4e75-bd82-1d73b8c291e9\"}\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:76:    raise SystemExit(\"manifest.files should list the 3 non-manifest emitted files; MANIFEST.json intentionally excludes itself from its own sha256 entries to avoid circular dependency\")\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:124:  [ -f \"$outdir/MANIFEST.json\" ] || fail \"missing manifest\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:143:  validate_manifest \"$outdir/MANIFEST.json\" \"$outdir\" \"$SCP_SHA\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"main\" \"false\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:169:  grep -Fq 'post-v1.2.0' \"$post_v120_stderr\" || fail \"post-v1.2.0 SHA did not warn\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:170:  grep -Fq 'TF-023E-002' \"$post_v120_stderr\" || fail \"post-v1.2.0 warning missing TF-023E-002 reference\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:171:  grep -Fq 'RECOMMENDED: use --scp-sha 41a529908ef5355b82ca924ef0502fa5ec2fcc11 (v1.0.0)' \"$post_v120_stderr\" || fail \"post-v1.2.0 warning missing recommendation\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:173:  validate_manifest \"$post_v120_outdir/MANIFEST.json\" \"$post_v120_outdir\" \"$SCP_SHA_POST_V1_2_0\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"main\" \"false\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:214:  ! grep -Fq 'post-v1.2.0' \"$missing_cutover_stderr\" || fail \"missing-cutover repo should not warn about post-v1.2.0\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:216:  validate_manifest \"$missing_cutover_outdir/MANIFEST.json\" \"$missing_cutover_outdir\" \"$cutover_sha\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"main\" \"false\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:258:  [ -f \"$outdir_non_head/MANIFEST.json\" ] || fail \"missing manifest for non-HEAD SHA\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:260:  validate_manifest \"$outdir_non_head/MANIFEST.json\" \"$outdir_non_head\" \"$non_head_sha\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"main\" \"false\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:275:  validate_manifest \"$outdir_master/MANIFEST.json\" \"$outdir_master\" \"$SCP_SHA\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"master\" \"true\"\ntests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh:289:  validate_manifest \"$outdir_develop/MANIFEST.json\" \"$outdir_develop\" \"$SCP_SHA\" \"jrnb2024/test-adopter\" \"develop\" \"false\"\ndocs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-correctness.json:1:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":786101,\"duration_api_ms\":786773,\"num_turns\":13,\"result\":\"I now have all the information needed. Let me compile the review JSON.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"r1-correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"CORRECTNESS\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024 024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-05-09\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Core logic is sound. The scaffolder argument validation, path-denylist canonicalisation, sed substitution, and MANIFEST emission are all correct. The adopter-wrapper template mirrors the canonical SCP-self wrapper shape — permissions correct (contents:read + statuses:write only), Renovate marker immediately before uses:, fork-PR refusal preserved, no id-token/attestations at workflow level. The check-invocation-log-entry.sh cascade-status extraction (exactly-one-match Python assert, set -e exit propagation), regex patterns for onboarded-operator-bump and blocked-on-adopter-conflict, adopter-slug canonicalisation, and all five check-paths are correctly implemented. Test coverage is broad: 6-case worked-example matrix, phantom-two-entry SAFE-003, exactly-one cascade-status assertion, slug canonicalisation case, no --restore coupling remaining. D-044 row format is correct, STATUS.md PM chain entry present (lines 139-150), plan-doc §6 split note present (line 213). One MAJ: the post-v1.2.0 warning test is unconditioned on v1.2.0 tag existence — it will spuriously fail in shallow-clone environments while masking the TF-023E-002 guard's reliability signal. Two MINs: the scaffold script's TF-023E-002 warning silently degrades when the v1.2.0 tag is absent, and the template first-line comment still says '024B' rather than '024B-core'. One nit: parse_entry_repo em-dash coupling to 024B-extras log emitter is documented but untested against real emitter output.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MAJ-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh post-v1.2.0 warning assertion at lines 169-172 is unconditioned on v1.2.0 tag existence; fails spuriously in shallow-clone environments while masking real guard failures.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Line 8: SCP_SHA_POST_V1_2_0=$(git rev-parse main). Lines 169-172 unconditionally assert TF-023E-002 warning fired. Scaffold script at lines 154-158 uses `git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null`; if v1.2.0 tag absent, command fails silently, warning not emitted, test fails at line 169 with 'post-v1.2.0 SHA did not warn'. No skip-guard present.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A developer with a shallow clone (or any environment missing the v1.2.0 tag) sees a spurious test failure with a misleading message. The failure mode looks identical to 'scaffold script TF-023E-002 guard is broken', making genuine regressions in that guard indistinguishable from environment issues.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Wrap the post-v1.2.0 assertion block in a tag-existence guard: `if git -C \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1; then <assert warning>; else echo 'SKIP: v1.2.0 tag not present; skipping TF-023E-002 warning test'; fi`.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh TF-023E-002 warning at lines 154-158 silently degrades to no-op when the v1.2.0 tag is absent from the SCP repo (e.g. shallow clone).\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"`git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null` — the 2>/dev/null swallows git's 'unknown revision' error when the tag is missing; the condition evaluates false; no warning emits. An operator on a shallow clone passes a post-v1.2.0 SHA, receives no TF-023E-002 alert, and may generate a startup-failing adopter wrapper.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Silent degradation of a safety warning. Operator on a shallow clone (e.g. fresh CI-assisted workflow authoring) gets no signal that their SHA will cause GitHub Actions startup_failure on adopter repos due to TF-023E-002.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Before the merge-base check, add a tag-resolve guard: `if ! git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>/dev/null; then warn \\\\\\\"v1.2.0 tag not found in SCP repo; TF-023E-002 check skipped\\\\\\\"; fi` — or emit the warning unconditionally for any SHA that is not V1_0_0_SCP_SHA, with a note to verify lineage manually.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl line 1 comment references 'WP-SCP-024 024B' instead of '024B-core' after the scope-split decision; every generated adopter wrapper carries inaccurate provenance.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Line 1 of the template: `# Generated by scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh = SCP-073-scaffolder per WP-SCP-024 024B (D-044).` The slice was renamed 024B-core per SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md. DISPATCH-NOTE confirms this is the '024B-core' slice.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Every adopter wrapper's provenance comment is slightly inaccurate. Auditors cross-referencing the comment against the git history or DECISIONS.md will find '024B' rather than '024B-core', adding friction at audit time.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change line 1 to `# Generated by scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh = SCP-073-scaffolder per WP-SCP-024 024B-core (D-044). Adopter wrapper canonical shape per ADOPT-001 §12.`\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"nit-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh parse_entry_repo hardcodes U+2014 em-dash as the log-header separator; coupling to 024B-extras log emitter is documented but untested against real emitter output.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"parse_entry_repo regex (line 248): `r\\\\\\\"^###\\\\\\\\s+.+\\\\\\\\s+—\\\\\\\\s+...\\\\\\\"` — literal U+2014. Comment: 'The separator is U+2014 (em dash); keep this in sync with the branch-protection log emitter in 024B-extras.' Tests use hardcoded fake-gh patches with em-dash. No test exercises the actual enable-required-check.sh log output.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If 024B-extras' log emitter uses a different separator (e.g. en-dash U+2013, ASCII hyphen, or --), parse_entry_repo will silently match 0 entries and die with 'expected exactly one log entry target match, found 0'. Will not be caught by 024B-core tests.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Accept as documented risk; add a TODO comment noting that 024B-extras must include an integration test verifying enable-required-check.sh's emitted log header matches parse_entry_repo's expected separator exactly.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\",\\n  \\\"fixpoint_gate\\\": \\\"0 CRIT + 0 MAJ required; currently 0 CRIT + 1 MAJ\\\"\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"729fc6e8-3771-46b3-a060-fceb51796d73\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.1659408,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":8,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":126074,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":275991,\"output_tokens\":40521,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":126074,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":8347,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":92862,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":40593,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":40593},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":2397,\"outputTokens\":26,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.0025269999999999997,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":8,\"outputTokens\":40521,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":275991,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":126074,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.1634138,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"a380539c-4303-4415-b2d1-87625fb4a8d4\"}\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:51:  shift 5 2>/dev/null || shift $#  # consume positional 1-5; remaining $@ are extra script flags (e.g. --allow-not-applicable)\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:620:  # Without --allow-not-applicable flag → exit 2 per fix-round-3 SAFE-MAJ-001 closure (technical guard for cohort-slice misuse).\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:621:  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 2 '' 'ERROR: cascade-status: not applicable found, but --allow-not-applicable was not passed.'\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:622:  # With --allow-not-applicable flag → exit 0 (tooling-slice opt-in). Pass empty 5th arg (default path) + the flag as extra arg.\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:623:  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 0 'OK: DISPATCH-NOTE declares cascade-status: not applicable; not a cohort cascade slice — nothing to enforce' '' \"\" --allow-not-applicable\ntests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:643:  run_case \"$repo_dir\" 2 '' \"ERROR: --allow-not-applicable was passed but cascade-status is 'onboarded'\" \"\" --allow-not-applicable\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:10:- **D-044** — reserved for slice 024B: `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` (`SCP-073-scaffolder`) operational contract + adopter-template versioning + invocation-log convention extension.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:16:- `WP-SCP-020` (Policy Federation Primitive) — closed 2026-04-30 at v1.0.0; v1.1.0 cut 2026-05-02; v1.2.0 cut 2026-05-03 (WP-SCP-023 023B emitter).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:33:5. Defines a **Threshold A criterion** for WP-SCP-024: ≥3 of the 5 cohort adopters have `policy-check / scp/policy-check` required-check live + ≥1 SCP minor version-bump cycle (e.g. v1.2.0 → v1.3.0) propagated cleanly **via Renovate** to those adopters (operator-driven bumps under R-024-07 do NOT count toward Threshold A on their own).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:71:8. **SCP version-skew tolerance is the cascade timing primitive.** Adopters cascade in onto a current SCP version (v1.2.0 as of 2026-05-03). The post-bake observation window (≥1 week per slice) is intentionally long enough that the next SCP minor version-bump (Renovate-driven SHA pin update) surfaces during the bake — proving version-skew tolerance against a real upgrade cycle, not just a single point-in-time onboard. A cascade slice that completes onboarding without a version-bump occurring during bake MUST extend bake until at least one Renovate-issued SHA pin bump has been merged + observed clean.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:102:| **T-024-05** | Cascade slice merges with a stale adopter wrapper SHA pin (e.g. v1.0.0 instead of v1.2.0), causing the adopter to miss safety fixes that landed at v1.1.0+. | Scaffolder template SHA pin. | Scaffolder (024B) generates wrappers pinned to the **current SCP main HEAD SHA**, not a hard-coded literal. Cascade slice's R1 review verifies the generated SHA pin matches the SCP main HEAD at slice-author time. Renovate-issued bumps catch any subsequent drift. |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:144:5. **FLA pilot safety findings reviewed** — single line in DISPATCH-NOTE (template lands in 024B's D-044): `FLA pilot safety findings reviewed: [none new since <last slice's check date> | <link to finding>]`. This is **not** an FLA precondition (invariant 4 still holds); it is a check that the cascade slice author has looked at the FLA pilot state before opening the slice, so safety discoveries don't silently accumulate between slices. Closes 024A R1 MIN-SAFE-005.\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:164:D-044 ratifies this script's operational contract: bootstrap-only (refuses CI=true / GITHUB_ACTIONS=true per D-035 symmetry), validates `--scp-sha` against the SCP main HEAD at invocation time (warns if it's not main HEAD; refuses to emit if it's a non-existent SHA), and emits a manifest file `MANIFEST.json` listing every file emitted + its SHA256 for audit. `MANIFEST.json local audit:` is the reframed form of the original §9 reservation language, which called for a rendered-template log entry alongside `branch-protection-log.md`; 024B-core reframed that audit surface on 2026-05-09 per `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md`. The local manifest provides the equivalent forensic-traceability surface without coupling to the branch-protection log (reserved for `enable-required-check.sh` per D-035, with the log entry remaining the operator's responsibility in 024C+).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:180:| **At 024C kickoff** | Estate cascade start announcement — names the 5-cohort, the canary-first sequencing, the post-bake SLO, the adopter-side rollback path. **Incorporates TF-023A-002** (the deferred WP-SCP-023 v1.2.0 release notification — re-deferred from 023D → 023E and now re-deferred again to here, since adopter participation actually starts with cascade). | `~/Projects/control-tower/governance/docs/notifications/SCP-ESTATE-CASCADE-START-2026-MM-DD.md` per `reference_ct_notifications.md`. | CT governance log is the canonical cross-repo conversation log per memory. |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:191:- ≥1 SCP minor version-bump cycle (e.g. v1.2.0 → v1.3.0) has propagated cleanly to those adopters via Renovate (proving version-skew tolerance per invariant 8 + T-024-04).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:206:| 024B | 024B-core: `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` + `templates/adopter-wrapper.yml.tmpl` + `scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh` + scaffolder unit tests | Scaffolder emits a known-fixture wrapper that schema-validates against the canonical wrapper shape from ADOPT-001 §12; `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` parses DISPATCH-NOTE `cascade-status:` field and implements all four CI behaviours per invariant 2 — **fail-closed default** (absent or unrecognised value → exit non-zero); for `onboarded`: requires log entry + target match; for `onboarded-operator-bump`: requires log entry + target match + `TF-024X-renovate-<adopter-slug>` STATUS.md row matching invariant 2's **regex format spec literally** (file-agnostic; `\\S` non-whitespace anchor at description start; ≥20 char total); for `blocked-on-adopter-conflict`: requires `TF-024X-conflict-<adopter>` reference (in DISPATCH-NOTE) matching the same regex spec literally AND **asserts log file was NOT modified** (closes the R3 safety bypass + R4 safety stub bypass + R6 safety regex/whitespace + file-scoping bypasses); D-044 filed. **024B-extras is deferred**: `enable-required-check.sh --restore` round-trip, ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass, workflow wiring, and step 7 operator demo move to the sibling slice. **024B-core is a hard predecessor for 024C; 024B-extras must also merge before 024C opens per the split decision.** | Codex executor | Per WP-SCP-022 dispatch contract |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:233:| **R-024-13** | Plan-doc commits to scaffolder shape before scaffolder is implemented. | Plan-doc is v0.1. Scaffolder's ratifying decision (D-044) lands at 024B with the implementation. Plan-doc shape may be amended at 024B if implementation surfaces a better shape. | 024A → 024B |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:242:- D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reserved (not assigned in this slice).\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:259:| **D-044** | 024B | `scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh` operational contract — bootstrap-only / no-CI guards (D-035 symmetry); `--scp-sha` validation against SCP main HEAD; `--default-branch` flag + template substitution; `MANIFEST.json` audit emission. Adopter-template versioning (template SHA captured in scaffolder output for forensic traceability). Invocation-log convention extension: scaffolder writes a \"rendered-template\" log entry alongside `enable-required-check.sh`'s branch-protection log. Ratifies invariants 2 + 3 + 10 in code form. |\ndocs/plans/WP-SCP-024-estate-cascade.md:263:**Codex-executor reservation guard.** Per the `docs/DECISIONS.md` header reservation block, Codex executors dispatched for any WP-SCP-024 implementation slice must NOT assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that slice. The reservation pattern mirrors WP-SCP-022's D-021 reservation guard + WP-SCP-023's D-041/D-042/D-043 reservation guard.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
